  "content": {
    "title": "Notre Dame's season ends with smiles and strength despite loss to UConn",
    "text": "March 29, 2026, 7:52 p.m. ETFORT WORTH, TX ― On Sunday, when the final buzzer sounded after the Notre Dame Fighting Irish lost to the UConn Huskies, 70-52, in the Elite Eight, there weren't many tears.Instead, Hannah Hidalgo gathered her teammates and encouraged them to huddle before they joined the already forming handshake line with UConn. Notre Dame coach Niele Ivey led Hidalgo and the rest of her players through the line as they congratulated UConn on their victory. When the Irish arrived at their postgame presser to talk about what happened on the floor at Dickies Arena, there were several smiles and a sense of gratitude.\"I feel like, just as a person, this team has made me so much better,\" Hidalgo said. \"Taking the team kind of on my back and just learning each and every single one of our players, who they are as a person, who they are as a player. I was a lot more intentional with that (this year).\"You know, they've helped me grow my patience and just my understanding, just the way I think. ... I'm so grateful for each and every single one of them. I wouldn't want to go to war with anybody else in this world.\"Ivey said she was proud of her team and the growth they showed this season, exceeding expectations along the way. Ivey said the transfers who joined her program enhanced the team's culture.She specifically called out Hidalgo as one of the best leaders she's had and pointed out Cassandre Prosper for being the most improved player in the ACC. Ivey also made sure to acknowledge KK Bransford, who shifted Notre Dame's trajectory when she returned from injury midseason.\"I'm so grateful and blessed that I get a chance to do what I love and in coaching this group,\" Ivey said. \"Obviously, we wanted to win this game, but for them to cement their legacy with me getting us back to the Elite Eight is something special, and I'll never forget it. This is one of my most special groups I've ever been around ... I love them so much.\"Ivey explained she loves coaching with joy, and this year's Notre Dame roster gave it back to her following last year's \"difficult\" season. She said the smile on her face was from being proud of her team because they proved themselves.Next season, the roster will shift again. Many of the eight seniors the Fighting Irish will likely move on, leaving Hidalgo to be the team's leader amid more changes. However, Hidalgo doesn't seem to be concerned. UConn coach Geno Auriemma revealed she's already planning how to beat the Huskies during her senior season.\"For people that are not familiar with South Jersey, that's just Philadelphia East, right? So (Hidalgo's) got a little bit of that toughness and grit ... and talent. It's rare.\" Auriemma said, praising Hidalgo.\"I just love watching her. I asked her after the game if she was old enough to go pro, and she said, 'No. I want one more shot at you guys.'\"",
